Rate Limiter:
    1. Token Bucket Algorithm -> centralized throttling
    2. Spring Cloud Gateway rate-limiter by YAML configuration. Internally use Token Bucket algorithm
       backed by Redis.
       ❌ Cannot express:
            10 req/sec per user AND 100 req/min per IP AND 500 req/min global
       For this Bucket4j (Redis): It is a rate limiting engine, not a gateway feature.
    3. Bucket4j (Redis): This is is a Java rate-limiting library based on the Token Bucket algorithm.
                This is NOT a gateway. It is in-process logic you embed inside your application.
    4. Circuit Braker. These are king of filters.
    5. Sliding Window Algorithm -> centralized throttling
    6. Leaky Bucket Algorithm




Token Bucket Algorithm:

    import java.time.Instant;

    public class TokenBucket {

        private final double refillRatePerSecond;
        private final double capacity;

        private double tokens;
        private long lastRefillTimestamp; // epoch millis

        public TokenBucket(double refillRatePerSecond, double capacity) {
            this.refillRatePerSecond = refillRatePerSecond;
            this.capacity = capacity;
            this.tokens = capacity;
            this.lastRefillTimestamp = Instant.now().toEpochMilli();
        }

        /*
            Here we use synchronized but this kind of setup will Works inside one JVM only.
            For distributed system we need to use Redis Lua. So, that all pods see same token bucket.
        */
        public synchronized boolean allowRequest() {
            refillTokens();

            if (tokens >= 1.0) {
                tokens -= 1.0;
                return true;
            }
            return false;
        }

        private void refillTokens() {
            long now = Instant.now().toEpochMilli();
            double secondsElapsed = (now - lastRefillTimestamp) / 1000.0;

            double tokensToAdd = secondsElapsed * refillRatePerSecond;
            tokens = Math.min(capacity, tokens + tokensToAdd);

            lastRefillTimestamp = now;
        }
    }

Spring Cloud Gateway – Rate Limiter (YAML):

    spring:
      cloud:
        gateway:
          routes:
            - id: user-service
              uri: http://localhost:8081
              predicates:
                - Path=/users/**
              filters:
                - name: RequestRateLimiter
                  args:
                    redis-rate-limiter.replenishRate: 10
                    redis-rate-limiter.burstCapacity: 20
                    redis-rate-limiter.requestedTokens: 1
                    key-resolver: "#{@userKeyResolver}"

      redis:
        host: localhost
        port: 6379

Explain:
    1. spring.cloud.gateway: Enables Spring Cloud Gateway. Gateway sits in front of microservices.
    2. routes:
      - id: user-service
                Unique identifier for this route.
                Spring Cloud Gateway rate limiting works per route.
                Internally, the filter is executed only when attached to a route.

    3. uri: http://localhost:8081. Target service where requests will be forwarded.
    4. User can write full path also.
        Example: http://localhost:8081/users
    5. predicates: Rate limiter Condition to match requests
    6. filters:
      - name: RequestRateLimiter
                Built-in Spring Cloud Gateway filter.
                The filter RequestRateLimiter. Uses RedisRateLimiter. This uses Lua script:
                    To Stores token count
                    To Refills tokens based on time
                    To Deducts tokens per request
                        All in one atomic Redis operation.
    7. args:
        redis-rate-limiter.replenishRate: 10
            Tokens added per second. Every second → 10 new tokens. Allows 10 requests per second steadily
        redis-rate-limiter.burstCapacity: 20
            Maximum tokens bucket can hold. Allows short bursts of traffic
        redis-rate-limiter.requestedTokens: 1
            Tokens consumed per request. Each HTTP request costs 1 token.
            In this above setup 21st request is throttled.

            For expensive API calls, we can
            increase this, so per request more token would be consumed and less load on server service.
                requestedTokens: 5


            Tokens are independent of request processing time, i.e, Request processing time is
            irrelevant for tokens.
            If we want to limit user request based on request - response duration:
                Semaphore-based limiting
                Bulkhead pattern (Resilience4j)


    8. key-resolver: "#{@userKeyResolver}":
            Determines who is rate-limited.
            Examples:
                Per user
                Per IP
                Per API key
                Per header

            For this we can create Bean also:
                @Bean
                public KeyResolver userKeyResolver() {
                    return exchange ->
                        Mono.just(
                            exchange.getRequest()
                                    .getHeaders()
                                    .getFirst("X-USER-ID")
                        );
                }
    9. redis:
            SpringCloudGateway service can have multiple Pods. And can spread over multiple clusters.
            But, still tokens are shared globally. For understanding, take this is a shared bucket
            and each SpringCloudGateway request can access this shared bucket across multiple pods and
            multiple clusters. All pods see same bucket.
            This all is possible with RedisRateLimiter.
                RedisRateLimiter guarantees atomic execution.
                request_rate_limiter.{routeId}.{key}

            Multiple Gateway Pods
                    ↓
            Shared Redis Cluster
                    ↓
            Lua-based Token Bucket

    Notes:
        We cannot switch algorithms (e.g. Sliding Window, Leaky Bucket) using
        Spring Cloud Gateway – Rate Limiter (YAML) directly.
        But this is still possible:
        As, Spring Cloud Gateway rate limiting works via:
            RequestRateLimiterGatewayFilterFactory
            RateLimiter<C>

            public class SlidingWindowRateLimiterConfig {
                private int maxRequests;
                private Duration window;
            }
            @Component("slidingWindowRateLimiter")
            public class SlidingWindowRateLimiter
                    implements RateLimiter<SlidingWindowRateLimiterConfig> {
            }

            Plug into YAML
              filters:
                - name: RequestRateLimiter
                  args:
                    rate-limiter: "#{@slidingWindowRateLimiter}"
                    maxRequests: 100
                    window: 1m

            How it works internally:
                Request
                  ↓
                KeyResolver
                  ↓
                RateLimiter.isAllowed()
                  ↓
                ALLOW → Forward request
                DENY  → 429 Too Many Requests

Bucket4j (Redis):
    Bucket4j is a Java rate-limiting library based on the Token Bucket algorithm.
    Bucket4j is NOT a gateway.
    It is in-process logic you embed inside your application.

    Bucket4j internal model:
        Bucket4j represents each bucket as:
            Bucket
             ├─ Bandwidth(s)
             │   ├─ capacity
             │   ├─ refill tokens
             │   └─ refill period
             ├─ current tokens
             └─ last refill timestamp

        Step 1: Identify the key.
        Step 2: Fetch bucket[From memory/Redis/Hazelcast]
        Step 3: Refill tokens.
                elapsedTime = now - lastRefill
                newTokens = elapsedTime * refillRate
                tokens = min(capacity, tokens + newTokens)
        Step 4: Try to consume.
                if tokens >= requiredTokens:
                    tokens -= requiredTokens
                    allow
                else:
                    reject
        Step 5: Persist state. Update tokens + timestamp

        Bucket state stored:
            Map<key, Bucket>
            Redis-backed Bucket4j:
                Bucket bucket = Bucket4j.extension(RedisBucketExtension.getInstance())
                    .builder()
                    .addLimit(Bandwidth.simple(10, Duration.ofSeconds(1)))
                    .build(redisProxy, "user:123");

        Bucket4j can tell client when to retry:
            probe.getNanosToWaitForRefill()
                Spring Cloud Gateway RedisRateLimiter cannot do this accurately.

        Where to place Bucket4j:    WHY
            Inside microservice:   Domain-specific limits
            Internal APIs:          Fine control




